 ## Background
-The concept of transforming SDFs via inverse matrices is a standard technique in ray marching, popularized by Inigo Quilez and others in the demoscene and real-time graphics communities [S35, unofficial]. Motion-compensated block warping for TSDFs is specific to 3D video compression and volumetric video streaming, where temporal redundancy is exploited by warping previous frames to predict current frames [S1, unofficial]. NTSL Marching appears in experimental graphics codebases (NTSL-graphics) focusing on deformed field integration [S1, unofficial].
+The concept of transforming SDFs via inverse matrices is a standard technique in implicit modeling, popularized by Ericson's *Real-Time Collision Detection* and widely adopted in real-time ray marching shaders [S2, unofficial]. Motion-compensated warping for TSDFs originates from video compression techniques adapted for 3D volumetric data, specifically in the context of dynamic 3D reconstruction and compression [S1, unofficial]. The NTSL Marching variant appears in experimental graphics codebases exploring deformed field integration [S1, unofficial].
 
